// Generated by tools/build_content.py. Part 2: Planning and architecture
export default {"lead": "<p>A database is designed top-down, from the real world to a conceptual model to a logical model to physical storage, and it is built in three layers so that a change in one layer does not break the layers above it.</p>", "secs": [{"id": "p2-data-as-a-resource", "t": "Data as a resource", "h": "<p>A <strong>resource</strong> is an asset that has value and incurs cost. A database qualifies: its operational data has value, it costs money to run, and it is professionally managed by a DBA. Data is the raw material for information, which in turn feeds knowledge.</p>"}, {"id": "p2-the-four-levels-of-data", "t": "The four levels of data", "h": "<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>What it is</th><th>Vocabulary</th><th>University example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Real world</td><td>The enterprise in its environment. The part the database represents is the <strong>mini-world</strong> (also called the <strong>universe of discourse</strong>).</td><td>Enterprise, mini-world</td><td>The university and its students</td></tr><tr><td>2. Conceptual model</td><td>The entities, attributes and relationships found in the mini-world</td><td>Entity, entity set, attribute, relationship; drawn as an ER, EER or UML diagram</td><td>\"Students enroll in classes\"</td></tr><tr><td>3. Logical model (the <strong>intension</strong>)</td><td>The structure of the database; relatively permanent</td><td>Metadata, record type, data item type, <strong>schema</strong> (stored in the system catalog)</td><td>The Student record type with stuId, name, major</td></tr><tr><td>4. Data occurrences (the <strong>extension</strong>)</td><td>The actual data stored at this moment; changes constantly</td><td>Data instance, record occurrence, file, database state</td><td>The 5,000 student rows in the table today</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Intension vs extension</strong> is a favorite test distinction. Intension is the definition (the empty table design). Extension is the contents (the rows that fill it right now).</p>"}, {"id": "p2-what-a-good-conceptual-model-looks-like", "t": "What a good conceptual model looks like", "h": "<ul><li>Faithfully mirrors the operations of the organization</li><li>Flexible enough to change as new information needs arise</li><li>Supports many different user views</li><li>Independent of physical implementation</li><li>Does not depend on the data model of any particular DBMS</li></ul><p>The conceptual model is the main communication tool between technical people and business people, because non-experts can read it.</p>"}, {"id": "p2-stages-in-database-design", "t": "Stages in database design", "h": "<ol><li>Analyze the user environment (interview users, study current reports and applications).</li><li>Develop a conceptual data model (entities, attributes, relationships: the ER diagram).</li><li>Choose a DBMS.</li><li>Develop the logical model (map the conceptual model to the DBMS's data model: tables and keys).</li><li>Develop the physical model (layout of data on the chosen hardware and software).</li><li>Evaluate the physical model (estimate performance; a prototype helps).</li><li>Perform tuning if the evaluation calls for it.</li><li>Implement the physical model.</li></ol><p>The stages are not strictly sequential. Any stage can loop back and force an earlier one to be revised. The practicum lives in stages 2 and 4.</p>"}, {"id": "p2-design-tools", "t": "Design tools", "h": "<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Data dictionary</td><td>A store of metadata. <strong>Integrated</strong> means it is the DBMS's own <strong>system catalog</strong>, always consistent with the real database. <strong>Freestanding</strong> means it is separate from any DBMS; useful early in design, but it can drift out of date.</td></tr><tr><td>Project management software</td><td>Charts, document control and communication for the design project</td></tr><tr><td>Diagramming tools</td><td>For drawing ER and UML diagrams</td></tr><tr><td>CASE tools (computer-aided software engineering)</td><td>Support analysis, design, and generating code from the model</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A data dictionary is used to collect information about data in one place, secure agreement on what each item means, communicate with users, track and assess changes to structure, and identify who is responsible for each item. It also exposes <strong>synonyms</strong> (different names for the same item, allowed if the system knows about them) and <strong>homonyms</strong> (the same name for different items, never allowed).</p><p>A <strong>data sublanguage</strong> has two parts: the <strong>DDL</strong> (data definition language) describes the database, and the <strong>DML</strong> (data manipulation language) processes it.</p>"}, {"id": "p2-the-database-administrator-dba", "t": "The database administrator (DBA)", "h": "<p>The DBA must be technically competent, a good manager, and a strong communicator, and has primary responsibility for planning, designing, developing and managing the operating database. A database designer may do the conceptual and logical design; the DBA does the physical design and implementation and then runs the system. Do not confuse the DBA with a Data Administrator, a strategic, less technical, usually executive-level role.</p>"}, {"id": "p2-the-three-level-architecture", "t": "The three-level architecture", "h": "<p>The standard architecture (from CODASYL DBTG and ANSI/X3/SPARC) describes a database at three levels of abstraction. The written description of each level is a <strong>schema</strong>.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><div class=\"scroll\"><svg class=\"dia\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 392\" role=\"img\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMin meet\" aria-label=\"Each level hides the one below it\" font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"24\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Each level hides the one below it</text><g><rect x=\"24\" y=\"48\" width=\"712\" height=\"100\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"var(--tint)\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"40\" y=\"74\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">External level (view level): what each user sees</text><rect x=\"40\" y=\"92\" width=\"208\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"276\" y=\"92\" width=\"208\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"512\" y=\"92\" width=\"208\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"144\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">View for user A</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">View for user B</text><text x=\"616\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">View for user C</text></g><g><line x1=\"380\" y1=\"148\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"204\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></line><text x=\"396\" y=\"172\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Logical data independence</text><text x=\"396\" y=\"189\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">views are immune to changes in the logical model</text></g><g><rect x=\"24\" y=\"204\" width=\"712\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"var(--accent)\" fill-opacity=\"0.12\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"></rect><text x=\"380\" y=\"229\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Logical level (conceptual level): the whole database</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"248\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">all entities, attributes, relationships and constraints; nothing about storage</text></g><g><line x1=\"380\" y1=\"264\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"320\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></line><text x=\"396\" y=\"288\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Physical data independence</text><text x=\"396\" y=\"305\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">the logical model is immune to changes in storage</text></g><g><rect x=\"24\" y=\"320\" width=\"712\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"380\" y=\"345\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Internal level (physical level): how data is stored</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"364\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">file organization, indexes, record sequencing</text></g></svg></div><figcaption>three-level architecture: external, logical, internal</figcaption></figure><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Also called</th><th>What it describes</th><th>Who sees it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>External</td><td>View level</td><td>Only the part of the database a particular user needs, in the form that user expects. There are many external views.</td><td>End users and their applications</td></tr><tr><td>Logical</td><td>Conceptual level, community view</td><td>The entire information content: all entities, attributes, relationships and constraints. Says nothing about how or where data is stored.</td><td>The DBA and designers</td></tr><tr><td>Internal</td><td>Physical level</td><td>How the data is actually stored: file organizations, indexes, record sequencing</td><td>The DBMS and operating system</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Why separate the levels:</p><ul><li>Different users need different views of the same data</li><li>A user's needs change over time</li><li>Users are shielded from the complexity of storage structures</li><li>The logical structure can change without affecting all users</li><li>Data and file structures can change without affecting the logical structure or users' views</li><li>The database structure is unaffected by changes to physical storage</li></ul>"}, {"id": "p2-data-independence", "t": "Data independence", "h": "<p>Data independence means upper levels are unaffected by changes to lower levels. It is the payoff of the three-level architecture.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example of a change that must not break anything above</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Logical data independence</td><td>Immunity of the external models to changes in the logical model</td><td>Adding a new table or a new column; existing users' views and programs keep working</td></tr><tr><td>Physical data independence</td><td>Immunity of the logical model to changes in the internal model</td><td>Moving to a new storage device, adding an index, changing how records are sequenced; the only visible effect is speed</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Physical data independence is the reason a user can ask for data by its logical name without knowing where or how it is stored.</p>"}, {"id": "p2-data-models-at-a-glance", "t": "Data models at a glance", "h": "<p>A <strong>data model</strong> is a set of tools, usually a diagram type plus a vocabulary, for describing the structure of a database.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Kind</th><th>One-line description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Entity-relationship (ER)</td><td>Semantic (captures meaning)</td><td>Entities, attributes and relationships; used for conceptual design</td></tr><tr><td>Relational</td><td>Record-based</td><td>Data and relationships stored as tables</td></tr><tr><td>Hierarchical</td><td>Record-based (legacy)</td><td>A tree; each node has exactly one parent</td></tr><tr><td>Network</td><td>Record-based (legacy)</td><td>Like a tree, but a node may have more than one parent</td></tr><tr><td>Object-oriented</td><td>Semantic</td><td>Objects with state (attributes) and behavior (methods); drawn in UML</td></tr><tr><td>Object-relational</td><td>Hybrid</td><td>Relational plus complex data types and methods</td></tr><tr><td>Star schema, data cube</td><td>Large-scale</td><td>A central fact table with dimension tables around it; used in data warehouses</td></tr><tr><td>Semi-structured, key-value, big table, graph</td><td>Large-scale</td><td>Flexible or schema-less models used in big data systems</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}]};
